<h1>Results</h1>

This section sketches how to reproduce the validation scenarios discussed in the
SEMA paper with this code. The program expects a parameter file on the command
line; if none is provided it will generate a default `parameters.prm` matching
the current dimension and co-dimension.

<h3>Setup checklist</h3>

- Choose dimension (`dimension`, `space dimension`) and mesh generators for
  fluid, solid, and tracer particles in `Navier-Stokes Immersed Problem/`.
- Pick material parameters (`Viscosity`, `Density`, `Lame mu`, `Lame lambda`)
  consistent with the intended test.
- Prescribe boundary data in `Navier-Stokes boundary conditions` and initial
  conditions for fluid and solid.
- Set `Number of time steps`, `Final time`, and `Output frequency`.


<h3>Compressed disk recovery</h3>

A neo-Hookean disk compressed to $\lambda_0 R$ is released in a quiescent box.
Record:
- Position of the disk center over time.
- Volume of the disk versus cumulative fluid flux across the open boundary.
- Sensitivity to mesh refinement (`Initial fluid/solid refinement`).


<h3>Annulus inflation with a point source</h3>

An annulus immersed in a box is inflated by a mass source at the center. Track:
- Growth of mean normal stress and deformation of the ring.
- Difference between injected fluid volume and solid volume loss as an error
  indicator for different refinement levels.


<h3>Cylinder and elastic flag benchmarks</h3>

The original FSI2 and FSI3 benchmarks are defined by Turek and Hron
@cite TurekHron2006. Both are two-dimensional channel-flow problems with an
elastic flag attached to a rigid cylinder; the numbers identify different
physical parameter sets. The channel is
$2.5\,\mathrm{m}$ long and $0.41\,\mathrm{m}$ high, the cylinder has radius
$0.05\,\mathrm{m}$ and center $(0.2,0.2)\,\mathrm{m}$, and the flag has
approximate length $0.35\,\mathrm{m}$ and thickness $0.02\,\mathrm{m}$.

The parameter file `turek_hron_fsi2_2d.prm` adapts this geometry and the FSI2
flow data to the present implementation. The native channel generator gives
a channel length of $2.2\,\mathrm{m}$, the flag is approximated by a rectangle,
and the single density parameter does not represent the original FSI2
solid-to-fluid density ratio of ten. Consequently, this configuration does
not reproduce the complete original benchmark specification.

For a finite-span three-dimensional flag, Section 5.1.2 of Failer and Richter
@cite FailerRichter2020 defines a beam of approximate dimensions
$0.35\times0.02\times0.20\,\mathrm{m}$ in a channel of depth
$0.41\,\mathrm{m}$, with $0.10<z<0.30\,\mathrm{m}$.
The file `turek_hron_fsi2_3d.prm` adopts this spanwise extent. Its channel
length, cylinder position, inflow profile, and material parameters retain the
present setup, so it is an adaptation of that geometry. The rectangular
approximation of the attachment to the cylinder is retained in both files.

The variants `turek_hron_fsi2_2d_parabolic.prm` and
`turek_hron_fsi2_3d_parabolic.prm` start with the quadratic velocity profile
$u_x=6\bar U y(H-y)/H^2$, with the other velocity components initially zero,
and prescribe the same inlet profile without a time ramp. In three dimensions
this profile is independent of $z$. After interpolation, the program distributes
the fluid constraints to impose the prescribed boundary values, including zero
velocity on the cylinder and channel walls, before storing the initial state
for the first time step. The original parameter files retain the start from
rest and the smooth inlet ramp. Each variant writes to a separate output
directory.


<h3>What to report</h3>

- Plots of the metrics above versus time, including convergence with respect to
  spatial refinements.
- Wall-clock timings from `TimerOutput` to compare assembly and solver costs.
- Notes on stability of the distributed Lagrange multiplier coupling for the
  chosen parameters.
